ch already conditions on `RandomUtils` itself and provides equivalent coverage.\n\n★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────\n- `condition.typeReached` must name a *loadable* class, matched against the regex `^[^.;\\[/]+(\\.[^.;\\[/]+)*(\\[])*$` — essentially dotted identifiers with optional `[]` array suffixes. JVM-generated lambda classes are named `EnclosingClass$$Lambda/0x<heap-address>` at runtime, which contains a `/`, so it can never satisfy that pattern — these names aren't meant to be referenced in source-level metadata at all.\n- The three near-duplicate entries are a tell-tale sign of an agent-generated artifact: each one came from a separate javaagent trace run, and the hex address after `/0x` is a JVM object-identity hash that differs every run — that's why \"the same lambda\" produced three different strings across captures instead of being stable.\n- Conditions in reachability metadata work by causality: if reaching class `A` implies some ancestor/enclosing class `B` was already initialized, conditioning on `B` is sufficient and strictly more robust. Since a lambda's enclosing method can't run before its enclosing class (`RandomUtils`) is initialized, the pre-existing `RandomUtils` condition already dominates the three bogus lambda-keyed entries — making them redundant as well as invalid.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────","ttft_ms":3033,"type":"result","duration_ms":79757,"uuid":"76d2718a-9ff1-4e4a-8f0c-3e1582502e4a","ttft_stream_ms":1746,"time_to_request_ms":102,"first_content_frame_ms":1747,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}

